  <section class="section" id="overview">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div class="tag">Research overview</div>
        <h2>What is architectural stabilization in LLM research?</h2>
        <p>
          Architectural stabilization explores how model structure, inference workflow, prompt scaffolding,
          validation layers, and system design choices can reduce volatility in outputs and improve reliability.
          Rather than treating failure as a single model issue, this research examines how stability can be created
          across the full interaction stack.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-3">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Problem space</h3>
          <p>
            LLM behavior can vary across similar prompts, contexts, and task conditions. This creates challenges in
            reproducibility, confidence, and downstream usability, especially in research or mission oriented
            settings.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Research lens</h3>
          <p>
            The project investigates stabilization as a systems problem. It considers architecture, orchestration,
            structured outputs, feedback loops, and evaluation strategy as interdependent components.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Expected value</h3>
          <p>
            A more stable LLM pipeline can support higher trust, stronger consistency, and clearer interpretation of
            experimental outcomes across multiple applications and environments.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="experiment">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div class="tag">Experiment and design</div>
        <h2>Experiment structure and evaluation logic</h2>
        <p>
          The experiment examines whether stabilization oriented design choices can improve output reliability under
          changing conditions. This includes structured comparison of task framing, architectural constraints,
          evaluation pathways, and consistency checks across repeated interactions.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-2">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Core experimental questions</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>How do architectural supports affect response consistency across repeated trials?</li>
            <li>Which design layers appear most influential in reducing instability?</li>
            <li>How do structured evaluation methods surface hidden variance or emergent failure patterns?</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Observed dimensions</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Reliability across similar prompts and tasks</li>
            <li>Structural consistency of outputs</li>
            <li>Robustness under context shifts</li>
            <li>Interpretability of behavioral changes across trials</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

